diff --git a/POLKIT.md b/POLKIT.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..3a591c7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/POLKIT.md
@@ -0,0 +1,272 @@
+# Polkit / pkexec support — design & progress
+
+This file tracks the work to add properly scoped permission handling via
+`pkexec` + a polkit policy, so Seekey can read `/dev/input/event*` through the
+`input` group without requiring the user to permanently join that group and
+without ever running the GUI as root.
+
+## Goal
+
+- Add a polkit policy that authorizes running the Seekey binary through
+  `pkexec`, after which the process immediately drops back to the invoking
+  user while keeping only one extra privilege: membership in the `input`
+  group.
+- Let the GUI configuration menu run without a second authentication once the
+  app is already running — **only if that can be done without reducing
+  security** (see decision D2 below).
+
+## Design
+
+### Privilege flow
+
+```
+ seekey (as normal user, no input access)
+   │  probe: do /dev/input/event* nodes exist that are
+   │  group-readable by `input` but not openable by us?
+   ├─ no ──────────────► run as today (no prompt)
+   └─ yes ─► pkexec <abs path to seekey> --seekey-restore-env=K=V ... <args>
+                │
+                ▼
+        seekey runs as root (pkexec, env sanitized by pkexec)
+          1. strip --seekey-restore-env args from argv
+          2. resolve PKEXEC_UID → passwd entry; resolve `input` → gid
+          3. initgroups() + setgroups(+input), setgid, setuid back to
+             the invoking user  (never runs as root past this point)
+          4. only NOW apply the saved session env vars, fix
+             HOME/USER/LOGNAME, chdir to the user's home
+                │
+                ▼
+        seekey as user + supplementary group `input`
+          → reads event nodes (udev rule: GROUP=input MODE=0660)
+          → GUI/Wayland/D-Bus work because the session env was restored
+          → every child it spawns (config menu, preview child, overlay)
+            inherits the `input` group for free
+```
+
+The unprivileged parent process waits for the `pkexec` child and exits with
+its status. If authorization is cancelled or denied (`pkexec` exit 126/127,
+or pkexec dying on a signal) the parent prints a warning and **aborts with
+exit code 2** — the probe had already proven that input access is
+unreachable without the `input` group, so the fallback overlay could only
+sit in "waiting for devices or permission changes" forever (decision D7).
+Spawn-level failures (no `pkexec` in PATH, spawn error) still continue
+unprivileged, because those are environmental problems rather than a
+refusal, and the existing udev/uaccess paths keep working.
+
+### Environment restoration (needed because pkexec sanitizes the environment)
+
+Verified against the polkit 127 `pkexec` source (the version shipped on the
+dev machine): pkexec runs the target with a minimal environment — it keeps
+only `SHELL`, locale vars, `TERM`, `COLORTERM`, `PATH`, `HOME`/`USER`/`LOGNAME`
+(of *root*), `PKEXEC_UID`, `SUDO_UID/GID`, and — only with the
+`allow_gui` annotation — `DISPLAY`/`XAUTHORITY`. Notably **`WAYLAND_DISPLAY`,
+`XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, `DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS` and all `XDG_*` base dirs are
+dropped**, which would leave a Wayland GUI unable to start.
+
+Mechanism: before spawning `pkexec`, Seekey appends
+`--seekey-restore-env=KEY=VALUE` arguments for an **allowlist** of session
+variables. The elevated process strips these arguments from `argv` while still
+root, and applies them to the environment **only after** it has dropped back
+to the invoking user — so no attacker-controlled environment value is ever
+consumed with root privileges. The allowlist covers: `WAYLAND_*`,
+`XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`/`XDG_CONFIG_HOME`/`XDG_DATA_HOME`/`XDG_STATE_HOME`/
+`XDG_CACHE_HOME`, `XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP`, `XDG_SESSION_TYPE`,
+`XDG_SESSION_DESKTOP`, `XDG_ACTIVATION_TOKEN`, `DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS`,
+`DISPLAY`, `XAUTHORITY`, `GDK_BACKEND`, `GDK_RENDERING_ENVIRONMENT`, `TERM`,
+`COLORTERM`. If `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` is still unset afterwards it is rebuilt as
+`/run/user/<uid>` when that directory exists.
+
+`allow_gui` is deliberately **not** set: it would make pkexec keep
+`DISPLAY`/`XAUTHORITY` in the *root* phase, which we do not need (the GUI is
+only touched after the drop), and the pkexec man page explicitly discourages
+it.
+
+### The polkit policy
+
+`data/dev.seekey.policy` is a template; `@BINDIR@` is substituted at install
+time so the `org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.path` annotation matches the real
+installed binary (pkexec matches the annotation against the *realpath* of the
+program and, when it matches, uses this action instead of the generic
+`org.freedesktop.policykit.exec`).
+
+polkitd only loads action files from a few fixed directories — upstream
+hardcodes `$sysconfdir/polkit-1/actions`, `/run/polkit-1/actions`,
+`/usr/local/share/polkit-1/actions` and its own datadir (no command line
+option, no environment variable; see `polkitbackendinteractiveauthority.c`)
+— and nixpkgs additionally patches in
+`/run/current-system/sw/share/polkit-1/actions`. There is no way to point
+pkexec at a policy that just sits in an arbitrary store path, so:
+
+- **Non-NixOS:** `sudo make install` / `./install.sh` already place the
+  policy under `/usr/local/share/polkit-1/actions`, which every polkitd
+  reads (D5).
+- **NixOS:** add seekey to `environment.systemPackages`. The system profile
+  aggregates `share/polkit-1/actions` from all packages, and the NixOS
+  polkit module sets `restartTriggers = [ config.system.path ]`
+  (`nixos/modules/security/polkit.nix`), so `nixos-rebuild switch`
+  restarts polkitd and it picks the policy up (fresh inotify watch too).
+  Reference this flake's package output from your config — same lock, same
+  store path — so the `exec.path` annotation also matches what
+  `nix run .#…` executes.
+- **Bare `nix run` without a system-profile install** keeps the generic
+  "Permission to run /nix/store/… as super user" dialog; that is purely a
+  packaging-path limitation, not a seekey setting.
+
+(A runtime workaround that self-installed the policy from the elevated
+process was implemented and then removed again once the system-profile
+path above was verified — see git history.)
+
+### Decisions
+
+**D1 — defaults: `allow_any=no`, `allow_inactive=auth_admin`,
+`allow_active=auth_admin`.**
+Administrator authentication is required for every elevation. No implicit
+authorization, no `yes`, no keep window. When the policy file is missing (or
+the path does not match, e.g. a build-dir binary), pkexec falls back to the
+generic `org.freedesktop.policykit.exec` action, which also requires admin
+auth — the flow degrades, it does not widen.
+
+**D2 — "no re-auth for the config menu" is achieved structurally, not with
+`auth_admin_keep`; the keep variant was omitted.**
+Once any process of the app has authenticated and dropped to
+user + `input`, everything it spawns — config menu, fuzzel session, GTK
+fallback menu, preview child, the overlay itself — is a child process that
+inherits the `input` group. No child ever calls `pkexec` again, so the
+in-app configuration menu (including its live preview) runs without any
+further prompt. The only case that prompts again is *starting a brand new,
+detached* Seekey process tree (e.g. the desktop "Open settings" action while
+the overlay was started separately) while no access is otherwise available.
+Making even that case seamless would require `auth_admin_keep` (a time-window
+during which any process of the session may re-trigger elevation without
+per-user interaction) or a custom IPC broker in the overlay. Per the task
+instructions — omit this if it cannot be done without reducing security —
+`auth_admin_keep` was considered and **omitted**: it trades a bounded but real
+security reduction for convenience in an edge case, while the requested
+in-app behaviour already works with plain `auth_admin`.
+
+**D3 — elevation is automatic, but only when it can actually help.**
+`seekey_input_group_access_would_help()` (in `src/input.c`) returns true only
+if at least one event node exists that this process cannot open, that is owned
+by group `input` with the group-read bit set, and that we are not already a
+member of. This avoids pointless authentication prompts when:
+
+- the udev/uaccess ACL already grants access (node openable),
+- there are no input devices at all (headless/VM),
+- the nodes are `root:root 0600` (adding the `input` group cannot help; the
+  existing "grant read access …" message stays correct),
+- the system has no `input` group.
+
+Elevation is never attempted for `--print-config`, `--validate-config`,
+`--init-config`, `--preview-child`, `--help`, `--version`.
+
+**D4 — `sudo seekey` keeps its legacy behaviour.** Only `pkexec` launches
+(`PKEXEC_UID` set and euid 0) trigger the drop-to-user + `input` group path.
+Running as root without `PKEXEC_UID` (the documented "quick test as root")
+is unchanged.
+
+**D5 — the policy is installed system-wide, even for `--user` installs.**
+`polkitd` only reads action files from the system data dirs
+(`/usr/local/share/polkit-1/actions`, `/usr/share/polkit-1/actions`), never
+from `~/.local/share`. `install.sh` already runs sudo for the udev rule, so
+the policy is installed with sudo in both modes; the annotation points at the
+actual `BINDIR/seekey`. Note: pkexec 127 does *not* verify root ownership of
+the target program, but older pkexec versions may reject a user-owned binary
+(`~/.local/bin/seekey`) — in that case pkexec fails and Seekey continues
+unprivileged with a warning (graceful degradation). `sudo make install`,
+AUR and Nix installs produce root/store-owned binaries and are unaffected.
+
+**D6 — translations.** The policy `<description>`/`<message>` carry an inline
+`xml:lang="zh_CN"` translation, matching the repo's convention of inline
+translations in `data/dev.seekey.desktop`.
+
+**D7 — a denied or cancelled prompt aborts the run (revised from the
+original "continue unprivileged" behaviour).** The probe only attempts
+elevation when the `input` group is provably required, so after a denial
+the overlay could never read input and would only show the permanent
+"waiting for devices or permission changes" state. `seekey_privilege_elevate`
+now returns -2 for a denial (pkexec 126/127) or for the elevated copy dying
+on a signal, and main() exits with code 2 after the explanatory message.
+Spawn-level failures keep the old continue-unprivileged behaviour: no
+pkexec binary or a spawn error is an environment problem, not a refusal,
+and the udev/uaccess fallback paths remain valid there.
+
+## Files
+
+| File | Change |
+|---|---|
+| `POLKIT.md` | this file |
+| `data/dev.seekey.policy` | new polkit policy template (`@BINDIR@` placeholder) |
+| `src/privilege.h`, `src/privilege.c` | new: pkexec drop, elevation spawn, env allowlist |
+| `src/input.c`, `src/seekey.h` | new probe `seekey_input_group_access_would_help()` |
+| `src/main.c` | drop at startup, elevation before mode dispatch |
+| `Makefile` | build `privilege.c`, install/uninstall policy (path substitution), policy validation in `check` |
+| `install.sh` | install/remove the policy (system-wide, sudo), usage text |
+| `package.nix` | `PREFIX=$out` so the annotation points into the store path |
+| `tests/test_privilege.c` | unit tests for argv stripping + env allowlist |
+| `README.md`, `README.zh-CN.md` | document the pkexec path |
+| `po/POTFILES.in`, `po/seekey.pot`, `po/zh_CN.po` | new `privilege.c` strings (zh translated) |
+
+The AUR `PKGBUILD`s need no change: `make DESTDIR=… PREFIX=/usr install`
+now installs the policy itself (annotation `/usr/bin/seekey`).
+
+## Progress
+
+- [x] Research pkexec 127 environment handling (verified against polkit source)
+- [x] Design decisions D1–D6 (above)
+- [x] `data/dev.seekey.policy`
+- [x] `src/privilege.h` / `src/privilege.c`
